# Item-slot indicator effects

Source proposal: the 2026-10-03 conversation proposing optional provider-registered effects, reusable deformable geometry, renderer-owned shader resources, and shared frame inputs.
Status: planning only; implementation and runtime validation are outstanding.

## Goal and scope

Allow an indicator provider registration to attach an optional rendering effect while preserving provider selection and cached fill/color sampling. Animate actual mesh silhouettes using reusable geometry and vertex shaders by default. Keep ItemSlotIndicatorSystem responsible for composition and selection, with rendering resource and animation responsibilities in separate owners.

Existing registrations retain their rectangular presentation. One minimal deformation effect verifies the extension boundary; selecting production themes for water, freshness, clothing, or other items is separate work.

Out of scope: arbitrary geometry-stage support, generated geometry per frame, per-item CPU simulation, effect stacking, public third-party extension compatibility, and redesigning provider priority or refresh policies. Do not introduce batching that changes GUI draw order. GPU execution alone is not a performance guarantee.

## Assumptions and implementation questions

- The effect attaches to the winning registration; the provider initially continues returning ItemSlotIndicator(Fill, Color). Per-item effect selection and dynamic provider-specific parameter payloads are deferred.
- Effect definitions describe immutable assets, geometry requirements, parameters, and motion requirements; they never own live GPU objects.
- The exact client frame callback, camera source, GUI transform, shader reload behavior, and state-restoration boundary require source verification before implementation.
- Establish bounded subdivision counts, shader input layout, motion units, and performance acceptance criteria before implementing their consumers. These values are not established by this plan.
- Live appearance and GPU timing require user-run acceptance. Headless tests cannot establish either; do not launch the game for visual acceptance.

## Phase 1: Establish rendering and compatibility contracts

Outcome: implementation has verified engine integration points and an explicit effect contract.

    ☐ Inspect ItemSlotIndicatorSystem, ItemSlotIndicatorRegistration, ItemSlotIndicatorRenderer, ItemSlotIndicatorPatch, existing providers, and their selection/scheduling tests.
    ☐ Verify the installed engine path used by the item-slot GUI hook, including GUI matrices, slot scaling, depth, blending, clipping, active shader, and item draw ordering.
    ☐ Identify a once-per-frame update boundary and authoritative camera orientation source; verify how repeated slot draws and multiple GUI surfaces share one frame snapshot.
    ☐ Verify engine shader registration, compilation, reload invalidation, and disposal behavior before defining resource replacement ownership.
    ☐ Define the common mesh/shader contract: normalized horizontal coordinate, bottom/surface edge identity, slot bounds, GUI transform, clamped fill, straight-alpha input color, time, motion, and immutable effect parameters.
    ☐ Define output constraints: slot containment, bottom anchoring, premultiplied output, zero-fill invisibility, full-fill coverage, and behavior near fill limits. Deformation must not turn empty/full values into misleading resource levels.
    ☐ Define finite-value handling, bounded subdivision counts, supported topology, parameter validation, and effect ID collision behavior.
    ☐ Establish a matched performance comparison workload and acceptance criteria, including visible slot counts, GUI scale, effect mix, CPU submission time, and GPU time.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Integration points and unresolved engine assumptions are recorded in this plan or the owning API documentation, with source references.
    ☐ Input/output and lifetime contracts are precise enough to implement without choosing production visual themes.

## Phase 2: Attach effects to provider selection

Depends on Phase 1.
Outcome: selection carries an optional effect without changing provider behavior.

    ☐ Add an immutable ItemSlotIndicatorEffectDefinition with stable ID, shader asset references, supported geometry description, bounded subdivision count, parameters, and motion-input requirement.
    ☐ Extend registration with an optional effect definition; existing registration calls remain valid and default to the plain rectangle.
    ☐ Introduce a render-selection value containing the sampled ItemSlotIndicator and the winning registration's effect definition.
    ☐ Keep IItemSlotIndicatorProvider.TryGetIndicator and its fill/color result unchanged; adapt selection consumers and tests deliberately without adding redundant forwarding APIs.
    ☐ Preserve descending priority, stable equal-priority ordering, absent-provider skipping, and zero-fill results as successful selections.
    ☐ Preserve weak sample ownership, context invalidation, refresh intervals, and adaptive sampling. Time and camera changes must not invalidate provider samples.
    ☐ Verify effect attachment belongs to the selected registration even when the indicator result comes from cache; verify default registrations retain ordinary rendering selection.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Focused selection and scheduling checks pass, including cached effect selection and zero-fill priority behavior.
    ☐ No provider owns shaders, meshes, rendering calls, or animation updates.

## Phase 3: Own reusable rendering resources

Depends on Phases 1–2.
Outcome: valid effect definitions resolve to shared, lifecycle-managed GPU resources.

    ☐ Add focused owners under ItemSlotIndicators/Rendering for effect programs and reusable meshes; retain one clearly named responsibility per file.
    ☐ Implement reusable quad and subdivided fill-strip geometry. Each strip sample has bottom and surface vertices using the established input contract.
    ☐ Cache meshes by geometry requirements and programs by effect identity; allow multiple provider registrations to share resources without duplicate compilation or uploads.
    ☐ Validate incompatible reuse of an effect ID deterministically; distinguish invalid registration data from runtime shader failure.
    ☐ Register and compile shader assets through the engine-owned shader API on the graphics thread. Prepare resources at initialization/registration or explicit reload boundaries, never in recurring slot draws.
    ☐ Handle reload by rebuilding resources under the verified engine ownership rules. Do not retain invalid program handles or assume engine reload preserves old programs.
    ☐ Isolate failures per effect, log once per load attempt, dispose partial resources, and retry only at an explicit load/reload boundary.
    ☐ Make disposal release each owned resource once and unsubscribe reload callbacks; support partial initialization and repeated cleanup safely.
    ☐ Verify shared-resource identity, failure isolation, and lifecycle behavior with focused checks; reserve actual compilation/link validation for a graphics context.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Resource ownership is explicit and recurring draws require no compilation, mesh generation, or mesh uploads.
    ☐ A failing effect does not invalidate unrelated effects or the existing rectangle resources.

## Phase 4: Supply shared frame inputs

Depends on Phases 1–2; may be developed independently of Phase 3.
Outcome: all indicator draws in a frame consume consistent animation inputs.

    ☐ Add ItemSlotIndicatorFrameState as the owner of animation timing and shared damped camera-motion state.
    ☐ Update at the verified frame boundary, never once per slot query or draw; expose a read-only snapshot for render consumers.
    ☐ Define motion axes, units, normalization, damping, and amplitude bounds; effects interpret the shared signal without introducing per-item CPU simulations.
    ☐ Handle angle wrapping, invalid input, first sample, frame-rate variation, long gaps, unavailable camera, and world/camera replacement without impulses or accumulated catch-up.
    ☐ Define animation clock precision and pause/resume behavior; avoid precision loss or discontinuities from unbounded float time.
    ☐ Avoid camera-dependent work when no registered effect needs it, while ensuring reactivation starts from a fresh baseline.
    ☐ Verify deterministic motion response and reset behavior with controlled inputs; verify repeated draws consume the same snapshot without advancing it.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Animation input production is independent of provider sampling and visible slot count.
    ☐ Missing camera input produces a defined neutral motion signal while time-only animation remains usable.

## Phase 5: Integrate effect rendering and rectangle fallback

Depends on Phases 2–4.
Outcome: an opted-in indicator can animate its silhouette behind the normal item draw.

    ☐ Extend the renderer to resolve the selected effect, bind shared geometry/programs, and submit the common inputs plus immutable effect parameters.
    ☐ Retain the ordinary rectangle path for registrations without effects and for unavailable/failed effects, using the same selected provider's fill and color.
    ☐ Never choose another provider or hide a valid indicator because its effect failed.
    ☐ Keep ItemSlotIndicatorPatch thin: select, render, then forward every original item-render argument unchanged.
    ☐ Preserve the current intended GUI layering, clipping, scale, and bottom alignment. Restore every modified surrounding render state, including active shader and depth-write/blend state, on success and exceptional exits.
    ☐ Apply the established fill/containment contract to effect geometry; avoid transparent fragments writing depth or changing neighboring slot appearance.
    ☐ Add one minimal deformation shader and an explicit development/test opt-in path to prove the boundary, without assigning production themes to existing providers.
    ☐ Verify the shader deforms surface vertices while retaining the bottom boundary; keep fragment shading simple and premultiply output exactly once.
    ☐ Define ownership/removal of any temporary diagnostic registration or instrumentation before shipping; retain reusable validation assets only with a clear purpose.

Completion criteria:
    ☐ Default and failure paths retain ordinary indicators and normal item rendering.
    ☐ The demonstration animates from frame inputs while cached provider data remains unchanged.
    ☐ No new per-frame mesh upload, shader compilation, or per-slot motion simulation occurs.

## Phase 6: Validate, review, and document

Depends on Phase 5.
Outcome: implementation evidence and its limits are explicit.

    ☐ Run builds and tests through subagents, as required by repository instructions; run focused indicator/provider regressions and relevant rendering-contract checks.
    ☐ Verify shader assets are included in the distributable and compile/link in the supported engine graphics context; distinguish package inspection from runtime shader validation.
    ☐ Obtain user-run appearance checks for inventory/hotbar placement, GUI scaling, scrolling/clipping, item overlays, empty/full/near-limit fill, camera movement, long gaps, and camera/world changes.
    ☐ Exercise failed effect loading, repeated shader reload, and shutdown; verify fallback, state restoration, bounded diagnostics, and resource cleanup in the graphics context.
    ☐ Measure matched rectangle/effect workloads at the agreed slot counts and GUI scales. Report CPU and GPU measurements separately, including shader switches and mesh-upload counts; do not claim GPU speedups from headless tests.
    ☐ Resolve regressions or explicitly record unmet acceptance criteria; optimize based on measurements without changing GUI draw order.
    ☐ Document provider registration, geometry/shader contracts, frame inputs, resource ownership, failure behavior, and how to add an effect.
    ☐ Ensure new classes/methods have language-appropriate documentation, nontrivial logic comments, and visibility-first method regions. Keep project staging terminology confined to planning documents.
    ☐ Perform a second implementation review and independent audit against this task list before marking completion; reconcile suspected issues against the full contract.

Final acceptance:
    ☐ Optional registration effects, shared geometry/programs, vertex deformation, shared frame inputs, fallback, and lifetime handling are implemented and verified.
    ☐ Existing provider selection, sampling, and unthemed rendering remain compatible.
    ☐ Required runtime evidence and matched performance results meet the established criteria; outstanding user-run checks remain explicitly unchecked.
    ☐ Production effect themes and geometry-stage support remain deferred rather than implicitly claimed as delivered.
